{
  "_note": [
    "Indian units and report conventions (V1 item 3.5). Used ONLY when comparing numbers (the Q&A and summary number guard, post-board adjudication). The value shown to the reader is always the value as written.",
    "families: each unit spelling with its factor to the family base unit (value x factor = value in the base unit). Spellings are matched case-insensitively; a space matches any spacing or a dot ('cu mm' matches 'cumm', 'cu.mm', 'cu mm'); 'µ' matches µ, μ, u or mc; '^3' matches ^3, **3, 3 or a superscript 3. A number written as '2.45 x 10^5' is multiplied out before the unit is read.",
    "Indian first: lakh(s) / lac(s) per cumm, gm%, cumm / cmm. A bare 'lakh' without '/cumm' is NOT a unit here (it is also a rupee amount).",
    "analyte_conversions: conversions that depend on the analyte (mg/dL to µmol/L). They are not exact (rounded factors), so they are compared with a 2 % tolerance and only when the analyte label (see analytes) is next to the number.",
    "analytes: label synonyms and the units customarily used for that analyte. A number with a label but no unit ('WBC 7.2') is compared with a quoted quantity of the same analyte expressed in one of these units only.",
    "Clinical team: add a spelling under its family, a synonym under its analyte, or a new analyte; keep factors exact and mark rounded conversions exact: false.",
    "units_per_volume (night-v3 item 2.1): enzyme activities and tumour markers or hormones in units. U and IU are written interchangeably on Indian reports for the same value (ALP 110 U/L = 110 IU/L); mIU/mL = IU/L and U/mL = kU/L exactly. A unit is never converted to a mass (AFP IU/mL to ng/mL only through its rounded analyte conversion). INR has no unit."
  ],
